<p>A shop's product cache runs on one <Term kind="redis">Redis</Term> server, with persistence turned off (<code>save ""</code>), as many caches do. One night the host is rebooted for a kernel patch. Redis comes back within a minute, but it comes back empty, and for the next few minutes nearly every product page misses the cache and reads from the <Term kind="database">database</Term>. The database, which normally answers a few dozen cache misses a second, receives well over a thousand. Pages slow down, some time out, and the on-call engineer watches the load fall back over about ten minutes. In the review the next morning, the team agrees on two actions: turn on persistence, and add a replica.</p>

<p>Both sound like obvious improvements, and both are less simple than they sound. Redis can save its memory to disk in two ways, keep copies on other servers, and fail over to a copy automatically. Each of these protects against a particular failure, costs something while nothing is failing, and can still lose data. Some of them can make a cache worse: a restored snapshot can bring back a price the application deleted an hour ago, and a replica can be wiped by the very server it was meant to back up.</p>

<p>This article goes through the mechanisms in order, from what losing the cache costs, through snapshots, the append-only file and replication, to failover with Redis Sentinel. I ran the experiments on Redis 8.10.3 on my laptop, and I say which numbers come from those runs and which from a model. Redis turns snapshots on by default, but my servers ran with persistence off unless a section says it turned it on. By the end you will be able to decide what a cache should run, what each choice costs the database when something fails, and which data in your Redis is not really cache and needs more protection.</p>

<h2 id="restart-cost">What losing the cache costs</h2>

<p>Every protection in this article has a price, so it helps to know first what it is protecting against. For a cache the answer is not lost data. Every value in a cache can be rebuilt from the database; that is what makes it a cache. The cost of losing it is the work of rebuilding it: every key that was a <Term kind="hit" mark>hit</Term> becomes a <Term kind="miss" mark>miss</Term> until it is loaded again.</p>

<p>A little arithmetic shows how large that is. If the cache's hit ratio is <em>h</em>, the database normally sees the fraction 1 − <em>h</em> of all reads. Straight after an empty restart it sees all of them, which is 1 ÷ (1 − <em>h</em>) times its normal load. At a 90% hit ratio that is 10 times, at 99% it is 100 times.</p>

<p>Figure 1 shows the shock over time. It models the shop's catalogue: 50,000 products whose popularity follows a Zipf curve (a few products get most of the views), 2,000 product reads a second, and cache-aside reads (from <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns">Caching patterns</ArticleRef>) that store each loaded product for a fixed time to live (TTL). It computes the expected database reads each second rather than simulating each request. Press Restart Redis to empty the cache.</p>

<RestartFigure number={1} />

<p>Before the restart, with copies kept for 10 minutes, about 96% of reads hit and the database sees about 73 a second. In the first second after the restart it sees about 1,330, eighteen times its normal load. That is less than the formula's 27 times because within that first second each popular product is loaded once, and later requests for it hit. The load falls quickly at first, as the popular products are reloaded, and slowly afterwards, because the long tail of rarely viewed products keeps missing; after a minute it is still more than three times normal. Leave the figure running. After about two and a half minutes the load dips below normal, because every popular product was loaded in the same few seconds and none of them expires until one TTL later; ten minutes after the restart they expire together, in a second, smaller wave. Spreading TTLs with a little random jitter, as <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> describes, flattens it.</p>

<p>Now change the TTL. Longer TTLs raise the hit ratio and lower the normal load, but the first second after a restart is the same 1,330 reads whatever the TTL. So a cache that works better is a bigger shock to lose, because the database has been sized for the small remainder: about 5 times normal load at a 2-minute TTL, about 50 times at 30 minutes. If your database could take the spike, losing the cache would be a non-event and you could stop reading here. Most databases behind a well-used cache cannot, which is why the shop's reboot hurt. <ArticleRef slug="stampedes">Cache stampedes</ArticleRef> explains why an overloaded database suffers more than the count of misses suggests, and how to limit the misses for any one key.</p>

<p>That gives three things a cache must not do, and I will measure every mechanism in this article against them. It must not lose everything at once when the database cannot absorb the misses. It must not serve values that should have been deleted, because a stale price is worse than a miss. And it must not refuse writes, because a refused <code>DEL</code> leaves a stale value behind. It may lose any single key at any time.</p>

<p>Some data kept in Redis is not cache in this sense: sessions, rate-limit counters, locks, queues, anything that exists only in Redis. Losing it is a real loss, not extra work for the database. Keep that data apart, ideally on its own Redis server, because the right settings for it are different, and I will point them out as they come up.</p>

<h2 id="rdb">Snapshots: fork and copy-on-write</h2>

<p>The first kind of persistence is the <strong>RDB snapshot</strong> (RDB stands for Redis database, the file format): a compact binary file holding every key and value as they were at one moment. The question is how Redis can write all of its memory to disk without stopping, since one thread runs every command, as <ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works">How Redis works</ArticleRef> explains.</p>

<p>The answer is that it does not write the file itself. On <code>BGSAVE</code>, or when a save rule fires, Redis calls <code>fork</code>, which creates a child process with a copy of Redis's memory. The child writes that memory to a temporary file, then renames it over the old <code>dump.rdb</code> and exits. Renaming is atomic, so the file on disk is always a complete snapshot, the old one or the new one. Meanwhile the parent, with the main thread, carries on serving clients.</p>

<p>The copy is not made in full. The operating system shares memory between parent and child, page by page (a page is usually 4 KB), and copies a page only when one of them writes to it. This is <strong>copy-on-write</strong>. Figure 2 shows it with 48 pages instead of millions. Press Start a snapshot, then watch what happens as the parent keeps handling writes.</p>

<CowFigure number={2} />

<p>Two costs appear. The first is a pause at the start. Forking does not copy the pages, but it copies the page tables that map them, and the parent's main thread stops while that happens. The Redis latency documentation lists about 10 ms per gigabyte on modern hardware, but hundreds on some older virtual machines. At 10 ms per gigabyte, a 25 GB cache pauses for a quarter of a second each time it saves. Check <code>latest_fork_usec</code> in <code>INFO</code> on your own servers.</p>

<p>The second cost is memory. Every page the parent writes to during the save exists twice, so the extra memory is the share of pages written while the child is running. Switch the figure between the two write patterns: spread out, five writes a second touch about 70% of the pages before the 12-second save finishes; on a few hot keys, only those keys' pages are copied. A real server takes thousands of writes a second and a save of a large dataset takes many seconds, so a cache with writes spread across its keys can need close to twice its memory during a save. On Linux, plan for that headroom, and set <code>vm.overcommit_memory</code> to 1 as the Redis documentation advises; Redis checks the setting and warns at start-up, because with the default the kernel can refuse the fork when memory looks short.</p>

<p>On Linux, <strong>transparent huge pages</strong> are a kernel feature that backs memory with 2 MB pages instead of 4 KB ones. After a fork, a write to a 100-byte value then copies 2 MB instead of 4 KB, which costs both memory and latency. The Redis documentation is blunt: they must be disabled. Redis 6.2 and later disable them for their own process (<code>disable-thp yes</code>, the default); on older versions, or if that setting is off, disable them in the kernel.</p>

<p>When does Redis take a snapshot? By default, with or without the shipped <code>redis.conf</code>, Redis 8.10 saves after an hour if at least one key changed, after 5 minutes if 100 changed, and after a minute if 10,000 changed (<code>save 3600 1 300 100 60 10000</code>). Many caches turn snapshots off with <code>save ""</code>, which the Redis persistence documentation notes is "sometimes used when caching".</p>

<h2 id="snapshot-restore">What a snapshot gives back</h2>

<p>After a crash, Redis loads the last snapshot on start-up. Everything written after it is lost. I checked this directly: of 1,000 increments to a counter, the 400 made after the last <code>BGSAVE</code> were gone after a <code>kill -9</code> and a restart (<code>rdb_changes_since_last_save</code> in <code>INFO</code> counts them). With the default rules that window can be up to an hour, which is why snapshots alone do not suit data that only exists in Redis.</p>

<p>For a cache, losing recent writes is harmless: they are rebuilt from the database. The surprise is the other way round. A snapshot also loses recent <em>deletes</em>, and for a cache a delete is how the application says that a value is wrong. Figure 3 shows a run I did by hand on my laptop.</p>

<SnapshotSequence number={3} />

<p>Step through it. The application deletes the kettle after the snapshot, exactly as <ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation</ArticleRef> recommends. After the crash the banner stays gone, because snapshots store each key's expiry as an absolute time and the banner's had passed. The kettle comes back at £39 with 3,573 seconds of TTL left. The delete has been undone, and nothing in the application knows.</p>

<p>The same thing happened to a second product that was re-cached at a new price after the snapshot: on restart it went back to its old price. So a restored snapshot drops keys whose TTL has passed, but brings back every other value as it was when the snapshot was taken. The staleness is bounded by the TTLs (another reason, beyond those in <ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation</ArticleRef>, to set one on every key), but with hour-long TTLs it can last an hour. If a snapshot is old enough that many of its values have changed since, starting empty is safer than loading it.</p>

<p>Two more behaviours of snapshots matter for a cache. First, if a background save fails, for example because the disk is full or the directory is not writable, and save points are configured (as they are by default), Redis refuses every write until a save succeeds. With the data directory made read-only, my <code>GET</code> still worked but <code>SET</code> and <code>DEL</code> were rejected with a <code>MISCONF</code> error. For data you must not lose, that is the right default: it makes a broken disk impossible to ignore. For a cache it is the wrong one, because every rejected <code>DEL</code> leaves a stale value in place. If a cache takes snapshots on save points, set <code>stop-writes-on-bgsave-error no</code> and alert on <code>rdb_last_bgsave_status</code> instead. With <code>save ""</code> and only an occasional manual <code>BGSAVE</code>, a failed save does not stop writes.</p>

<p>Second, loading takes time, and Redis is unavailable for it: every command except a few administrative ones is answered with a <code>LOADING</code> error until the file has been read. On my laptop a million small keys (100-byte values) took about half a second to load when it was quiet, and 2 to 5 seconds when it was heavily loaded. It depends on hardware and value sizes, so expect hundreds of milliseconds to seconds per million keys, and measure yours. That time counts against the snapshot when you compare it with starting empty, as the last section shows.</p>

<h2 id="aof">The append-only file</h2>

<p>The second kind of persistence records every write rather than the state at one moment. With <code>appendonly yes</code>, Redis appends each write command to the <strong>append-only file</strong> (AOF), in the same protocol format clients use, and replays the file on start-up. Relative expiries are rewritten as absolute ones as they are logged: my <code>SET product:2 v2 EX 60</code> appeared in the file as <code>SET product:2 v2 PXAT 1793141198378</code>, a time in milliseconds. So an AOF, like a snapshot, does not bring back keys whose time ran out while Redis was down.</p>

<p>The file grows with every write, so Redis periodically <strong>rewrites</strong> it: a forked child writes the current dataset as a compact base file, and new writes go to a fresh incremental file. Since Redis 7.0 the AOF is a directory of such files tied together by a manifest. A rewrite forks, so it has the same pause and copy-on-write cost as a snapshot.</p>

<p>How much an AOF can lose depends on when the data reaches the disk. Writing to a file only puts the data in the operating system's page cache, in memory. It reaches the disk when Redis calls <strong>fsync</strong>, the system call that forces a file's data from the page cache to the disk, or when the operating system decides to flush it. Figure 4 follows each <Term kind="write" mark>write</Term> from the application to the disk. Choose an fsync policy, then press Crash Redis, and then Cut the power.</p>

<AofFigure number={4} />

<p>A crash of the Redis process usually loses nothing acknowledged, because each write has already been handed to the operating system, which keeps running and writes it out. I checked this: with the default policy, 1,000 increments and a delete, then <code>kill -9</code>, and every write was there after the restart. The exception, which the figure leaves out, is <code>everysec</code> on a slow disk. While an earlier fsync is still running, Redis holds new writes in its own memory for up to two seconds and replies to clients anyway, so a crash in that window loses them. Cutting the power is different, because the page cache goes with it. With <code>appendfsync everysec</code>, the default, Redis fsyncs about once a second, so it can lose about the last second of acknowledged writes, or two on a slow disk. With <code>no</code> it leaves flushing to the operating system, which on Linux commonly means up to about 30 seconds. With <code>always</code> it fsyncs before replying to each write, so nothing acknowledged is lost, and every write waits for the disk.</p>

<p>That wait is expensive. On my laptop, 50 clients sending <code>SET</code> managed about 59,000 a second with <code>everysec</code> and 4,600 with <code>always</code>; macOS exaggerates the gap, but expect <code>always</code> to cost a large part of your write throughput on any system.</p>

<p>For a cache, an AOF is rarely worth having. It protects writes that the database already holds, and its value after a restart is the same as a snapshot's: a warm cache that may contain values the application had since deleted. It adds a way to refuse writes, too: with the AOF on, a failed write to the file, from a full disk say, makes Redis refuse every write, <code>DEL</code> included, and no setting turns that off. It earns its place for the data that is not really cache. If a counter or a session store must survive a power cut, Redis 7.2 added <code>WAITAOF</code>, which blocks until a write has been fsynced locally, on replicas, or both. With <code>everysec</code> it waits for the next fsync, so expect up to about a second; on my loaded laptop <code>WAITAOF 1 0 2000</code> took a median of 0.88 seconds. Use it for the few writes that matter, not for every cache fill.</p>

<h2 id="replication">Replicas: copies on other servers</h2>

<p>Persistence protects against a restart. It does nothing when the host itself is lost, and it does not shorten the time Redis is unavailable; it only changes what Redis holds when it comes back. For those you need a second server already running with a copy of the data. Redis provides this with <strong>replication</strong>: one server, the <strong>primary</strong>, accepts writes, and one or more <strong>replicas</strong> keep copies of its data.</p>

<p>A new replica starts with a <strong>full synchronisation</strong>. The primary forks and produces a snapshot, sends it to the replica, which loads it in place of whatever it held, and then streams every write it receives after the snapshot. By default the snapshot goes straight down the network connection without touching the disk. A replica rejects writes from clients by default, with a <code>READONLY</code> error.</p>

<p>After that, the primary sends every change down a continuous <strong>replication stream</strong>. Each server counts the bytes of stream it has processed, its <strong>replication offset</strong>, and the stream as a whole is named by a <strong>replication ID</strong>, a random string the primary creates. A replica with the same ID and offset as its primary holds the same data.</p>

<p>The important word in the Redis documentation is asynchronous. The primary replies to the client as soon as it has applied a write, and sends the write to its replicas afterwards; replicas confirm what they have processed about once a second. So a replica is always slightly behind, and a read from a replica can return a <Term kind="stale" mark>stale</Term> value, one the primary has already changed. Figure 5 shows this. A writer changes one key twice a second on the primary; readers read it from the replica. Use the slider to change how long the replica takes to apply each change.</p>

<LagFigure number={5} />

<p>The badge on each server shows the version of the key it holds, and the readouts show the two offsets and how far apart they are. At 1 ms almost every read is current. At 250 ms about 40% of reads return the previous version, and at 3 seconds nearly all of them do. The share follows from the write rate and the delay alone. A read is stale if at least one write arrived in the last <em>d</em> seconds. With writes arriving at random, <em>w</em> a second, the chance that none did is e<sup>−<em>wd</em></sup>, so a read is stale with probability 1 − e<sup>−<em>wd</em></sup>.</p>

<p>On one machine the delay is tiny. When I sent a <code>SET</code> to the primary and immediately read the key from the replica, 2 reads in 5,000 were stale; reading 1 ms later, none in 2,000 were. Across a network, under a write burst or while a replica is busy, the delay is longer. So treat replica reads as eventually consistent: usually stale for milliseconds after a write, but with no upper bound, on top of the staleness <ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation</ArticleRef> describes. Serving reads from replicas spreads load, but when one application instance writes and then reads its own write, that read should go to the primary.</p>

<p>The milliseconds become minutes when a replica loses its primary. By default (<code>replica-serve-stale-data yes</code>) it keeps answering reads from whatever it holds, while no deletes arrive. When I froze a primary, its replica reported the link as up and served the old value for the whole 60-second <code>repl-timeout</code>, then marked the link down and went on serving it. With <code>replica-serve-stale-data no</code>, a replica whose link is down answers <code>MASTERDOWN</code> instead, which a cache-aside client can treat as a miss; but mine still served for those first 60 seconds, not yet knowing the link had gone. So on replicas that serve reads, watch two fields of <code>INFO replication</code>: <code>master_link_status</code> and <code>master_last_io_seconds_ago</code>. The second normally stays at 10 or less, because the primary pings its replicas every 10 seconds, and it is the only sign of a silent primary in that first minute.</p>

<p>If a write must reach a replica before the application carries on, the client can send <code>WAIT 1 100</code> after it: wait up to 100 ms until at least one replica has confirmed it, and reply with how many did. On my laptop that took a median of 0.76 ms. <code>WAIT</code> tells you about the writes you have sent so far on that connection; it does not make Redis strongly consistent. Its own documentation says so, and the section on failover shows why.</p>

<h3 id="replica-expiry">Expiry and memory on a replica</h3>

<p>Replicas do not expire keys on their own. A key is deleted on a replica only when the primary expires it and sends a <code>DEL</code> down the stream; this keeps every copy identical. Until then, the replica hides keys that are past their TTL from reads. I froze a primary (with <code>SIGSTOP</code>) just after it set a key with a 3-second TTL: 4 seconds later the replica answered <code>GET</code>, <code>EXISTS</code> and <code>TTL</code> as if the key were missing, yet still counted it in <code>DBSIZE</code>. Promoted instead, the replica expired the key itself at once.</p>

<p>So a replica never serves an expired value, but its memory can hold expired keys for as long as its primary is slow to send deletes. The same is true of eviction. A replica ignores its own <code>maxmemory</code> by default (<code>replica-ignore-maxmemory yes</code>) and evicts only when the primary sends deletes. A replica I gave a 5 MB limit held 14.87 MB with nothing evicted. Give replicas the same memory as the primary, plus room for the stream they buffer during a full synchronisation (next section), since a replica that runs out of memory cannot simply evict its way out.</p>

<h3 id="backlog">When the link breaks: the backlog</h3>

<p>Networks drop connections. When a replica reconnects, it does not want a full synchronisation of a large dataset if it has only missed a few seconds of writes. So the primary keeps the most recent part of the stream in a fixed-size ring buffer, the <strong>replication backlog</strong>. A reconnecting replica sends its replication ID and offset (the <code>PSYNC</code> command); if the bytes it is missing are still in the backlog, the primary replies <code>+CONTINUE</code> and sends just those, a <strong>partial resynchronisation</strong>. If they have been overwritten, it falls back to a full one. Figure 6 does the arithmetic for 5,000 writes a second.</p>

<BacklogFigure number={6} />

<p>The default backlog is 1 MB, which at that rate holds about a second and a half of writes. I saw both outcomes on my laptop: 277 KB of missed stream gave a partial resynchronisation, 2.8 MB a full one. A full synchronisation of a large cache is expensive: the primary forks, the snapshot crosses the network, and the replica blocks while it loads. So size the backlog for the longest break you want to survive: write rate in bytes a second times that time. Memory is usually cheap enough to set it to tens or hundreds of megabytes. A full synchronisation has a limit of its own: the writes that arrive while the replica loads are buffered, on the replica since Redis 8.0 and then on the primary, up to <code>replica-full-sync-buffer-limit</code> and <code>client-output-buffer-limit replica</code> (256 MB each by default). A large, busy cache that exceeds the limits has its replica dropped and the synchronisation started again, so raise them for such caches.</p>

<p>How does a primary know that a replica has gone, rather than being quiet? Through <code>repl-timeout</code>, 60 seconds by default, the same timeout a replica applies to its primary. I froze a replica: about a minute later the primary dropped it, and when I resumed it, it reconnected within the backlog with a partial resynchronisation.</p>

<p>A primary can also be told to refuse writes when its replicas fall behind, with <code>min-replicas-to-write</code> and <code>min-replicas-max-lag</code>. With <code>min-replicas-to-write 1</code> and the default lag of 10 seconds, freezing the only replica made every write fail with <code>NOREPLICAS</code> about 10 seconds later. That limits how much a primary can accept that no replica has seen. For a cache it has the same flaw as <code>MISCONF</code>: the refused writes include deletes. Use it only for data that must not be lost, and keep it off a cache.</p>

<h2 id="sentinel">Failing over with Sentinel</h2>

<p>A replica holds a copy, but nothing yet switches to it when the primary fails. Something has to notice, choose a replica, promote it to primary, point the other replicas at it, and tell the clients. Redis does this with <strong>Redis Sentinel</strong>: a separate process, usually three or more of them on different machines, that watches a primary and its replicas. (Redis Cluster has its own failover built in; <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys">the next article</ArticleRef> covers it.)</p>

<p>Each Sentinel pings the primary every second. When one has had no valid reply for <code>down-after-milliseconds</code>, it marks the primary <strong>subjectively down</strong> (SDOWN): down in its own view. It asks the other Sentinels, and when at least <strong>quorum</strong> of them agree, the primary is <strong>objectively down</strong> (ODOWN). One Sentinel then asks to be elected leader for this failover, and needs the votes of a majority of all Sentinels. The leader picks a replica, sends it <code>REPLICAOF NO ONE</code> to make it the primary, and reconfigures the other replicas to replicate from it. Figure 7 runs that sequence in real time. Press Kill the primary and watch the Sentinels' pings go unanswered.</p>

<SentinelFigure number={7} />

<p>With the default setting, nothing changes for 30 seconds. Every client request <Term kind="error" mark>fails</Term>, and the Sentinels are simply waiting: the primary might be in a long pause, or the network might be dropping a few packets, and a failover is disruptive enough that Sentinel waits to be sure. Then everything happens in about a second. The figure uses the delays from my own run. Counting from the kill, the Sentinels marked the primary subjectively down at 30.07 to 30.18 seconds, objectively down at about 30.2, elected a leader at 30.3 and promoted the replica at 31.1. My client, sending a request every 100 ms, failed for 31.1 seconds. With <code>down-after-milliseconds</code> set to 5 seconds, the same client was back 6.3 seconds after the kill. Both runs were on one machine with the process killed, so connections were refused at once; when a whole host dies, clients also wait for their connection timeouts.</p>

<p>So the default costs about half a minute of unavailability, and you can shorten it by lowering one setting. The price of a short setting is false alarms: a primary that stops answering for a few seconds, through a network blip or a long pause, is failed over while it was healthy, and clients pay for another failover. For a cache I would choose a few seconds: a needless failover costs a cache little, and 30 seconds of unavailability can cost the database more than an empty restart would (see the last section).</p>

<p>Which replica is promoted? Sentinel first excludes any replica that has been disconnected from the primary for too long (roughly ten times <code>down-after-milliseconds</code>). Among the rest it prefers the lowest <code>replica-priority</code>, then the replica that has processed the most of the stream, then the lowest run ID (a random identifier each Redis process picks at start-up). A replica with priority 0 is never promoted, which is useful for a replica kept in another region for reading only.</p>

<p>The quorum and the majority answer different questions, and Figure 8 lets you see both. The quorum is how many Sentinels must agree that the primary is down. The majority, more than half of all Sentinels, is what it takes to authorise a failover. If the quorum is set higher than a majority, it is needed for the authorisation too.</p>

<QuorumFigure number={8} />

<p>Try two Sentinels with a quorum of 1 and one of them lost. The survivor can see that the primary is down but cannot fail it over, because it needs two votes. I ran three Sentinels and stopped two: the remaining one logged the primary as subjectively down but never objectively down, and no failover happened. So use at least three Sentinels, on three machines that fail independently. Five survive the loss of two.</p>

<p>The clients have to find the new primary. A client that supports Sentinel is given the Sentinels' addresses rather than the primary's, and asks them for the current primary with <code>SENTINEL get-master-addr-by-name</code>. The Redis documentation for client authors says to check the answer by sending <code>ROLE</code> to that server, and to ask again whenever a connection fails or a server reports itself as a replica. When Sentinel reconfigures a server, it also makes that server drop its client connections, so clients reconnect and ask again. A client given a fixed address instead keeps using the old primary: it gets connection errors while that is down, and <code>READONLY</code> errors once it is back as a replica, until the client is restarted.</p>

<p>For planned work, such as the kernel patch in the opening, do not kill the primary and wait for detection. Since Redis 6.2 the <code>FAILOVER</code> command hands the primary role to a replica deliberately: the primary stops accepting writes, waits for the replica to catch up, and then swaps roles with it. When I ran it under continuous writes it took 881 ms, and every write the old primary had acknowledged was on the new one. Under Sentinel, use <code>SENTINEL FAILOVER</code> instead, so that the Sentinels and their clients follow the change. It starts a failover at once, without waiting for the primary to be marked down, but it does not first wait for the replica to catch up as <code>FAILOVER</code> does, so a delete in the last moment can still be lost, as the next section shows. For a cache, a TTL on every key and a replayable outbox cover that window.</p>

<p>With a single server and no replica, <code>redis-cli SHUTDOWN SAVE</code> before the reboot writes a snapshot as Redis exits, even with <code>save ""</code>, so it restarts warm. The costs are the load time and the failed requests while it is down, and any delete attempted in that time is not applied.</p>

<h2 id="failover-loss">What a failover loses</h2>

<p>Replication is asynchronous, so at the moment a primary dies there can be writes it has acknowledged that no replica has received. When a replica is promoted, those writes are gone. For a cache, lost writes of values do not matter; lost deletes do. Figure 9 shows a run I did with a primary and one replica, and a second way to lose a delete that I come back to below.</p>

<FailoverSequence number={9} />

<p>To make the replica fall behind I froze it, standing in for a busy replica or a slow network, while the primary acknowledged 9 MB of writes and then the <code>DEL</code> of the kettle. <code>WAIT 1 500</code> after the delete returned 0: no replica had it. I killed the primary, and the promoted replica returned the kettle at £39 with almost an hour of TTL left.</p>

<p>Nothing in the application's logs records this: the delete succeeded. That is why <ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation</ArticleRef> recommends that deletes that must not be lost come from an outbox or change log that can be replayed after a failover, and a TTL on every key so that nothing lost lasts long. <code>WAIT</code> after each delete narrows the window but cannot close it: when it returns 0 you know the delete is at risk, but the primary has already applied it. Its documentation calls it best-effort for this reason.</p>

<p>A network partition loses deletes another way. Switch Figure 9 to the primary being cut off: application servers that can still reach the old primary keep writing to it during the failover, and it discards those writes when it rejoins as a replica. The Sentinel documentation notes that for a cache this can even be useful, since the cut-off clients keep working. Writes of values lost this way are harmless; deletes are not, and the same remedies apply. For data that must not be lost, <code>min-replicas-to-write</code> limits how long the isolated primary keeps accepting writes, at the price of refusing writes when replicas lag.</p>

<h3 id="fast-restart">When a replica makes things worse</h3>

<p>One combination makes a replica useless at exactly the moment it is needed: a primary with persistence turned off that restarts automatically. Figure 10 shows two runs with three Sentinels at their default settings. Choose what happens after the primary crashes.</p>

<WipeSequence number={10} failoverFigure={7} />

<p>If a supervisor such as systemd restarts the primary at once, it is back in under half a second, empty and with a new replication ID, long before any Sentinel has waited 30 seconds; their log recorded only a reboot. The replica cannot continue a stream the primary no longer knows, so it takes a full synchronisation of the empty dataset. In my run, with two replicas, both were empty within 10 seconds of the crash, while every Sentinel still reported the original primary as healthy. If the primary instead stays down, Sentinel promotes a replica with all its keys.</p>

<p>The Redis replication documentation warns about exactly this, and advises that a primary without persistence should not restart automatically. For a cache there are three ways out. Do not restart a crashed primary automatically, and let Sentinel fail over. Or give the primary persistence so that it restarts with data, with the stale-delete risk of Figure 3. Or accept that a fast restart empties both servers, and do not count the replica as protection against it. Managed services have the same trap: Amazon ElastiCache's documentation says that when you reboot a primary yourself, which does not trigger a failover, it comes back cleared of data, and the replicas then clear their copies.</p>

<h2 id="choosing">Choosing what a cache should run</h2>

<p>Now compare the choices on what matters for a cache: how hard the database is hit, and for how long. Figure 11 uses the model from Figure 1. Redis fails, stays unavailable for a while, and comes back either empty or with its data, as it does after loading a snapshot or when a replica is promoted. While it is unavailable, the application sends every read to the database. The dotted line is the reference: Redis restarting empty at once. Which choice is better depends on how much the database can take, so the figure lets you set that too.</p>

<CompareFigure number={11} />

<p>Start with a database that can take 500 reads a second, about seven times its normal load. Restarting empty at once keeps it above capacity for about 19 seconds. Coming back with the data costs nothing once Redis is back, but while Redis is unavailable the database receives every request, 2,000 a second, so every second of unavailability is a second above capacity. Counted in seconds above capacity, a replica promoted after 6 seconds is better, though the overload is deeper while it lasts: 2,000 reads a second, against the empty restart's peak of about 1,330. One promoted after about 31 seconds, what I measured with Sentinel's default 30-second <code>down-after-milliseconds</code>, is worse in this model than an empty restart. So is a snapshot that takes half a minute to load, which is also staler.</p>

<p>Now lower the capacity to 300 reads a second. The empty restart keeps the database overloaded for 45 seconds, and the 31-second failover becomes the better choice; the two cross at about 380, five times normal load. Less traffic moves the crossing the same way, because the cache refills only as fast as requests arrive: with half the requests and a database half the size, an empty restart stays above capacity for 37 seconds. So make the comparison with your own numbers. At every capacity in the figure, though, a 6-second failover is never meaningfully longer above capacity than an empty restart, and coming back empty after a minute, as when a host is lost and a new one has to start, is far worse than either.</p>

<p>The model leaves out two things that push in opposite directions. It does not slow the database down when it is overloaded, which in practice makes every second above capacity worse and favours the faster recovery. And it treats the application as sending reads to the database while Redis is down; if your application instead fails the request, the cost is errors rather than database load, and the comparison is in failed requests instead.</p>

<p>From the figure and the earlier sections, a reasonable default for a cache whose database cannot take a cold start is:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Persistence off on every server (<code>save ""</code>, <code>appendonly no</code>), or snapshots with <code>stop-writes-on-bgsave-error no</code> if you want a warm restart and accept that deleted values can return for up to a TTL.</li>
  <li>One replica, with the same memory as the primary and a replication backlog sized for the breaks you expect.</li>
  <li>Three Sentinels on separate machines, with <code>down-after-milliseconds</code> of a few seconds, and clients that discover the primary through Sentinel.</li>
  <li>No automatic restart of a primary that has no persistence.</li>
  <li><code>SENTINEL FAILOVER</code> (or <code>FAILOVER</code>, without Sentinel) before planned maintenance, so a kernel patch never becomes an empty restart.</li>
  <li>An outbox or change log for deletes that must survive a failover, and a TTL on every key.</li>
</ul>

<p>A replica doubles the memory you pay for. The same money buys other protection. Splitting the cache across two primaries means losing one server loses half the cache, not all of it, so the spike in Figure 1 halves; <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys">the next article</ArticleRef> is about splitting. A database with headroom, or a cache that can be rebuilt quickly in a controlled way, may need no replica at all; <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache">Operating a cache</ArticleRef> covers warming a cold cache. And if your database can take a cold start, the cheapest cache is one server with persistence off.</p>

<p>Data that is not really cache needs the opposite answers: AOF with <code>everysec</code> at least, <code>WAITAOF</code> or <code>WAIT</code> for writes that matter, perhaps <code>min-replicas-to-write</code>, and the knowledge that Redis replication can still lose acknowledged writes at failover. Put it on separate servers so the cache does not inherit those costs.</p>

<p>Managed services package these choices. Amazon ElastiCache with Multi-AZ promotes the replica with the least replication lag and says writes can resume "typically just a few seconds" later, the fast end of Figure 11, with the same asynchronous replication and lost writes; for Valkey 9.0 and later it also offers a durability mode for data that must not be lost. Valkey, the open-source fork of Redis, shares the mechanisms in this article, which I ran on Redis, but check its defaults for the version you run.</p>

<h2 id="decisions">What you can now decide</h2>

<p>Back to the shop's kernel patch. The right fix is not persistence and a replica as such. It is a replica with Sentinel, failed over deliberately before the reboot with <code>SENTINEL FAILOVER</code>, so the cache never goes cold; Sentinel tuned to detect a real crash in a few seconds; and no automatic restart of a primary without persistence. Persistence is optional for a cache, and comes with a risk the team had not considered: a warm restart can serve prices they deleted.</p>

<p>The same two questions apply to any setup you meet. How much can the database take if this server comes back empty? And could a restore or a failover bring back a value the application deleted?</p>

<p>Everything here has assumed that the whole cache lives on one primary. When it outgrows one server, or one key becomes too hot for any server, the data has to be split across several primaries, each with its own replicas and failover. <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys">Redis Cluster and hot keys</ArticleRef> picks up from here.</p>

